#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cedd027b-bceb-5728-9d11-42c9a3c4c015" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the ODE Solved by the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/id #uuid "795c8239-e963-51fe-9829-49040b556231" :question/math-academy-id "e-21770" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x),$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(9{C}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =9{C}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{9{C}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^2 = 9C^2\\tan(x)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nC^2 = \\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{9\\left(\\dfrac{y^2}{9\\tan(x)}\\right)\\sec^2(x)}{2y}\n= \\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{y\\sec^2(x)}{2\\tan(x)}\\right)}\n= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -\\dfrac{2\\tan(x)}{y\\sec^2(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since we divide by $y$ when forming the orthogonal slope, the orthogonal-trajectory ODE is only valid where $y\\neq 0$ (and where $\\tan(x)$ is defined). In particular, the original family forces $y=0$ whenever $\\tan(x)=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/id #uuid "86abbc30-d597-5461-9adf-dec505ebf3f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21771" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2},$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nC = x^2y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{2C}{x^3} = -\\dfrac{2x^2y}{x^3}\n= -\\dfrac{2y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{2y}{x}\\right)}\n= \\dfrac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int 2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int 2y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n2y^2 - x^2 = K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/82/82bd1506c86aff662ba47618c0a6cdaf8ed1db3fa617be301fcb2d318373a913.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\neq0,$ we have $y\\neq0$ on every curve in the family, so slopes $y'$ and $'_\\perp$ are well-defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091fb33a-0ae9-5b6f-b944-c5047129533a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374801} {:answer/id #uuid "d027cb04-90fe-522b-b6f5-3473a3cb33c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374802} {:answer/id #uuid "baabcc94-39c3-5d6f-a95c-51afa2f628c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374803} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9b7c20-41c6-5fa4-a52e-08948672a8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374804} {:answer/id #uuid "8899f6cb-db9f-5a42-9d08-928f71dcefaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8899f6cb-db9f-5a42-9d08-928f71dcefaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374805} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b37d6fc-fa8e-5feb-aa49-91d50d51b274" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56a4ae78-a8bc-54b5-b1dd-521d2b8670ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-336599" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}+C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}+C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({x}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}+C) \\\\ 2x & =2y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{x}{y})}=-\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{x})\\,dx \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,dy & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ xy & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=\\pm {e}^{{K}_{1}}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}+C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\nxy=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7f/7f85729bb9cc69865971fc11e1398738326bbc79235ea0e31c162ea1980e6d33.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $y$ and $x,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15dba9ae-15c6-5ce1-8ff4-9e54c7022329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}+x=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501510} {:answer/id #uuid "765226f3-3037-5537-a83b-e10c4e998f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-x=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501686} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e3e84b-ebf5-571e-96f7-0d88af45ad6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501774} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fa4360-f70a-5a96-86a1-e097d08537de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+{e}^{2x}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501783} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c90516-6e2c-58c1-9c24-827c686e2047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+x=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7c90516-6e2c-58c1-9c24-827c686e2047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+x=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501792} :answer-field/id #uuid "e519677f-c9c3-5941-8d8f-ba3066c6d529" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "835838f0-dbf2-5a34-824d-954c2e06b295" :question/math-academy-id "q-336616" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $Cy={e}^{2x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(Cy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({e}^{2x}) \\\\ C{y}^{′} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\nCy={e}^{2x}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y})}=2y.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-1 \\\\ \\int 2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-1)\\,dx \\\\ \\int 2y\\,dy & =-\\int dx \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+x & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+x=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e9/e941d35788f4aee837220962511ca537528b04dec9efaa7f956a4ee571e14189.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family, so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cedd027b-bceb-5728-9d11-42c9a3c4c015" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the ODE Solved by the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121dd7b4-2544-54e9-b82c-91427f263603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501496} {:answer/id #uuid "17ac5bc2-5b2c-5952-9696-90205b58421a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501512} {:answer/id #uuid "69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501661} {:answer/id #uuid "711d3051-555f-5251-b3e7-5ba581b6dcac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501674} {:answer/id #uuid "dba0a80a-5b15-55dd-8f84-6cb26b9b34c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69daf29a-b23e-53d1-ac1f-ef8529b1e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501661} :answer-field/id #uuid "60260284-1b87-5a9c-95ee-efdd32c08bc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0166ce2f-2829-5697-886e-176606ebec4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337311" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{1}{C}) \\\\ 2xy+{x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cedd027b-bceb-5728-9d11-42c9a3c4c015" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the ODE Solved by the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5310661c-c40f-57d8-961e-c3c723529252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501618} {:answer/id #uuid "80e52af3-e585-5a0d-aa22-4ea4ca9a03f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501713} {:answer/id #uuid "8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501738} {:answer/id #uuid "e51a135b-6f9a-5f82-ace0-3594f1b83adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501929} {:answer/id #uuid "f1216dab-2435-53da-876f-2a411b9c344f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d206d52-d945-58d6-9564-2bab92e38fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501738} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6a4d2e-c7d0-561a-ba37-0ecf2d6f5d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f66e36cd-9bf6-56e0-b87a-016d297333d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337313" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cedd027b-bceb-5728-9d11-42c9a3c4c015" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the ODE Solved by the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04836823-2fb1-5c3d-a0c5-77a932219b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501476} {:answer/id #uuid "0761bbe1-fc86-571b-915d-d390a147033c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501484} {:answer/id #uuid "6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501643} {:answer/id #uuid "a8371a77-d019-545f-94c5-55f27b9ed753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501795} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0edf3-56a1-540a-ba78-687280e03d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6054dcca-ae62-54cf-9cdd-57ed58a6a320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501643} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2e9024-0792-5fc6-9ce4-361c190fbfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc13bee1-bf2a-51a5-a80d-77b695b1c60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337315" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C,$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(C) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cedd027b-bceb-5728-9d11-42c9a3c4c015" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the ODE Solved by the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4893c99a-7136-5bd1-aef4-b6e90b029371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501600} {:answer/id #uuid "531fe1e5-e3d5-5649-b313-13cd6d8f7f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501619} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501669} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0fae8f-9d6c-5176-a980-fcb53f0d4c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501850} {:answer/id #uuid "dde6aaf3-b2a8-57d1-a021-79d4beafe63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d0e9c64-8b2c-5046-9a49-1b2ece1ed25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501669} :answer-field/id #uuid "f427581a-7c85-5678-8de9-9e496c491b50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3933c79b-01f9-56d3-9429-e1ebc3d9a1ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-337317" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C),$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C)) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x>0,$ $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\ne 0,$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119965 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cedd027b-bceb-5728-9d11-42c9a3c4c015" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the ODE Solved by the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f439cf56-cadd-5440-9e4e-4cb1193e0b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374891} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd73af6-a6e1-539d-a010-2a13d927834b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374892} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2caae4-d1d1-56e3-ba73-717a0a9a2e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374893} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9b6079-f9fd-51b8-b5ff-31df4c6dd3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374894} {:answer/id #uuid "b2122dfe-4ca3-58cb-9f80-d44f2dc84d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e9b6079-f9fd-51b8-b5ff-31df4c6dd3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374894} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d574f0-74bd-5e05-b29e-d5cd37a5199b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d9a7bf7-c360-5b6d-8323-d7ffd06fced2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337322" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{C}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{2}=-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2(-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4})}{{x}^{2}y}=-\\frac{y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{2x})}=\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: For the original family, since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ whenever $x\\ne 0,$ hence, the slopes are well-defined. Otherwise, when $x=0,$ the curve is not defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "833addb7-0164-5f7c-bb34-29651996fbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374912} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7ee723-e1fc-538d-a9fe-fe42e7426afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374913} {:answer/id #uuid "75811765-47db-5662-b560-125a09e37856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374914} {:answer/id #uuid "e791fc1a-5891-5df4-bee0-fa25c7da72b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374915} {:answer/id #uuid "1faceebb-b201-5935-b797-b0dcbc04a0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e791fc1a-5891-5df4-bee0-fa25c7da72b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374915} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba512409-31cf-5e9d-99b6-4d1f84e3bf75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20e84823-52da-5d8d-9751-7e5bcade7fd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-337569" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ 2x+2y{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{x}{y})}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,dy & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & =K \\\\ y & =Kx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=\\pm {e}^{{K}_{1}}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\ny=Kx,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca980af3c5db940a9ab95567dfaa1a69ea8afb9f4f42f772bb446df67df129b8.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $y$ and $x,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01243280-a8f6-5503-94bb-a930a8a2f0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501466} {:answer/id #uuid "24e069a3-4467-5da5-aa50-4d23695fc6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501533} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9be344-8141-5390-b7cb-280192fa2e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501640} {:answer/id #uuid "850f976f-2f35-5bd0-8500-d963993c3749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501721} {:answer/id #uuid "c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c320f8ae-2bdc-5750-bd04-80ef25d8f08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501854} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c26a07-893b-5eda-88b9-2f21c73d94f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33804b3c-aea9-58d0-9654-8a84f729c14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337573" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3854e6c47f28ce601ef2395379bea788e954c4e4057331772b5840cd7073237.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f607760-f38e-5bf2-a1b4-0d34c34ea859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374926} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea651f5-4f21-5ab8-bf09-afef1ae55fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+Cx=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374927} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b0da75-edae-5175-8a8e-f4eaa45c5472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374928} {:answer/id #uuid "8d745ed3-1b2b-5076-b4de-ea32211caf25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374929} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbc2b8e-411f-5505-b619-5bd2cb1e86c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bbc2b8e-411f-5505-b619-5bd2cb1e86c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374930} :answer-field/id #uuid "080d2f6c-6aee-5b11-b159-6789b41fc162" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1112c629-7ea8-5085-813a-086cecee9fd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337598" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=Cx,$ where $C\\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=Cx.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(Cx) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =C \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{C}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=Cx\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})}{2y}=\\frac{y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{2x})}=-\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-2x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-2x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-2\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-{x}^{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+2{x}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=Cx,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/039a42b728cad07b3e8f5dfd2f1607b36819e586f40e54e06d942679bb4ededa.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family (except possibly at $x=0$), so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined away from $y=0.$"}]}
